The arrow represents $\underset{\sim}{b}$, a displacement of $17$ cm on a bearing of $125^{\circ}$.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"vector-definition-notation theory.workedExamples[1].question_text; ordinary arrow and labels black; unshaded directed segment"}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (-35:3.8);
\fill (P) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (Q) circle (1.6pt);
\draw[->,thick] (P)--(Q) node[midway,above right] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\node[above left] at (P) {$P$};
\node[below right] at (Q) {$Q$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Write the vector in point-to-point notation.

The vector $\overrightarrow{CD}$ represents a displacement of $9$ cm west.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw[->,thick] (C)--(D);
\path (C) node[below] {$C$};
\path (D) node[below] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Describe what $\overrightarrow{CD}$ means.
(b) State what the arrow's length and arrowhead represent.
